 Local object pagination prepares one disk-backed manifest before opening maintenance database transactions. This first step visits the directories once (O(n) time, bounded memory); subsequent batches seek directly to a byte offset and inspect at most 1,000 candidates. Preparation may take time on a large volume and requires roughly 73 bytes of temporary disk space per ordinary object per active scan. Startup cleanup retains its independent 1,000-entry cap and does not prepare a manifest. Pass `next_object_cursor` back through `--object-cursor` to continue across process restarts. Deletion does not require adjusting the cursor. Old `{shard,offset}` cursors safely start one fresh scan. Manifests live in `.object-scans-v1`, expire after seven days, and allow at most four unfinished scans per volume; finish an existing scan instead of repeatedly starting another. Completed manifests remain retryable until a new scan reclaims them. An expired/missing cursor requires restarting without a cursor. Interrupted preparation is rebuilt on retry. New or concurrently changing objects may wait for the next full scan; current file age/type, references, active upload guards and publication pins are rechecked before deletion. Portable object backups exclude these rebuildable manifests. GCS/S3 continuation tokens and object paths are unchanged.
 
+Preparation collects candidate names without per-entry type queries; the current regular-file and directory-link checks run when each page is consumed. Startup requests metadata only for temporary-upload candidates within its 1,000-entry enumeration allowance. Manifest writes use one bounded 256 KiB buffer. Pages use the immutable manifest's known length instead of explicitly reading ahead to test for another page. These reduce work while retaining the existing cursor format, manifest byte layout, reference checks and upload guards. They do not remove the initial linear scan or its temporary disk requirement.
+
 `backup.sh` defaults to `BMT_BACKUP_MODE=online`. It acquires the shared GC guard before `pg_dump`, streams immutable objects, writes checksums/completion, then releases the guard. A crash leaves GC paused, while API and workers continue. Recover using the private `GC_RECOVERY_TOKEN` with `python -m bmt_backend.backup_guard end --token <saved-token>`; the token must match the active guard. The completion marker is written after successful cleanup; guard-release or writer-restart failures keep it absent. Rehearse restore into a different empty project with the matching image/schema before switching traffic. The task tested pg_dump/archive/restore with live synthetic writes; the complete Compose wrapper needs target-host acceptance.
 
 The optional `compose.cloud.yaml` selects an explicitly configured private HTTPS S3-compatible endpoint, bucket/prefix and credentials. Use it only after provider integration, access controls and backup/restore have been tested. `BMT_COMPOSE_CLOUD=1` adds that override to backup/restore helper commands. Provider backups stream bounded verified objects into the same portable archive; restore to a fresh **local** object volume (`BMT_COMPOSE_CLOUD=0`) first, verify hashes/PBs, then rehearse a separate verified transfer to the chosen provider before enabling its runtime configuration. Credentials are supplied privately and never included in artifacts. Default NAS configuration retains the local adapter and isolated worker network.
